{"title":"2024 USAJMO Problems/Problem 4","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Another_way_to_prove_that\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Another way to prove that</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_USAJMO_Problems/Problem_4&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/f/1/8f1e2533d6abdb11a9a1626c0240fab1a9200fc6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n \\ge 3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"43\" height=\"15\" /> be an integer. Rowan and Colin play a game on an <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/d/c3d38f82f48ef9e81c04d49354293305b0067afc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n \\times n$\" width=\"43\" height=\"9\" /> grid of squares, where each square is colored either red or blue. Rowan is allowed to permute the rows of the grid, and Colin is allowed to permute the columns of the grid. A grid coloring is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/3/c/73c1c51a8773cf07ff1bce73f34e7d712fd6b89f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$orderly$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"61\" height=\"18\" /> if:\n</p>\n<ul><li>no matter how Rowan permutes the rows of the coloring, Colin can then permute the columns to restore the original grid coloring; and</li></ul>\n<ul><li>no matter how Colin permutes the columns of the coloring, Rowan can then permute the rows to restore the original grid coloring;</li></ul>\n<p>In terms of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />, how many orderly colorings are there?\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_USAJMO_Problems/Problem_4&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We focus on the leftmost column for simplicity. Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/0/f5047d1e0cbb50ec208923a22cd517c55100fa7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"10\" /> be the number of red squares in this column. We then have five cases:\n</p><p><br />\n1. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/4/b/a4b5800f5ad040d308609190ef18a9e0ca08a9b0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m=1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"51\" height=\"14\" />\n</p><p>When Rowan permutes the rows of the coloring, we consider only the first column, which by the above contains <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/4/b/a4b5800f5ad040d308609190ef18a9e0ca08a9b0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m=1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"51\" height=\"14\" /> red colors, so there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/4/6/b462fcebf7579b00d34a4fd2521c294993962d29.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"${n \\choose 1}=n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -23px\" width=\"76\" height=\"55\" /> ways to permute the first column’s rows. Thus every other column will have to contain one different permutation of the first column; otherwise, there will be at least one permutation of which there is no corresponding column.\n</p><p><br />\nFurthermore, each permutation will be different, so each row will contain one and only one red square, which also fulfills the case of if Colin permutes the coloring first. Thus there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/e/c/3ece3d3ad1eb6327e78a4c52135d0f147e138691.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n\\cdot (n-1)\\cdot(n-2)\\cdot\\cdot\\cdot2\\cdot1=n!$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"255\" height=\"20\" /> different colorings for this case (the same as choosing squares such as no square is in the same row or column as any other square).\n</p><p><br />\n</p><p>2. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/f/6/af644ae994acd3a0a7d07b14d53601d1eeed4a77.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m=n-1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"84\" height=\"15\" />\n</p><p>This is essentially the same as case 1 except for the coloring; now there is one blue square and the rest are red squares. Thus there are also <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/3/f/63f344e7d8b14a7886fc7fb72da53cd0a3271dcc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n!$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" /> different colorings for this case.\n</p><p><br />\n</p><p>3. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/e/2/2e275495e7e44d279fb3e5051b3a63115e79ead5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m=0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"51\" height=\"14\" />\n</p><p>Since we have an entirely blue column, we are unable to have a column with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> red square only as doing so would leave one permutation that is not covered by at least one column (that space is being taken for the blank column). We are also unable to have a completely blue column as doing so would allow for Colin to shift the columns and in doing so fail for Rowan to shift back the columns. We also cannot have a column with any other number of red squares other than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/1/bc1f9d9bf8a1b606a4188b5ce9a2af1809e27a89.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> as will be shown below, so there is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> case here in which the entire coloring is red.\n</p><p><br />\n</p><p>4. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/e/c/fec4f3da4f422b2346efe86724c8a0aee1023fac.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m=n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"53\" height=\"10\" />\n</p><p>This is the same is an entire blue column, and, similar to above, we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> coloring.\n</p><p><br />\n</p><p>5. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/9/3/893a4bf02b38064f66836477b7b62af906f476cf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1&lt;m&lt;n-1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"118\" height=\"15\" />\n</p><p>This is the final case and is equivalent to permuting for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/e/aded525343d735dc4710dd8fef45c50b9e87eed8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"${n \\choose m}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -23px\" width=\"46\" height=\"55\" /> different ways. We must prove that this is greater than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> to show that the columns are not able to contain every possible permutation of this column for all values of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/0/d/70d8a348130d140aad5a9c23958578539f753241.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n&gt;3$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"43\" height=\"13\" /> (when <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/6/3/5635737307f5f0651cced8ee2e6558a426fd27b5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n=3$\" width=\"43\" height=\"12\" />, there is no such positive integer <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/0/f5047d1e0cbb50ec208923a22cd517c55100fa7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"10\" /> that satisfies the conditions). Note that if we have any column with a different number of red squares, it is an unattainable column and is thus not optimal.\n</p><p><br />\nLemma: Given that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/0/f5047d1e0cbb50ec208923a22cd517c55100fa7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> are positive integers such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/9/3/893a4bf02b38064f66836477b7b62af906f476cf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1&lt;m&lt;n-1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"118\" height=\"15\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/0/d/70d8a348130d140aad5a9c23958578539f753241.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n&gt;3$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"43\" height=\"13\" />, it is true for all <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/0/f5047d1e0cbb50ec208923a22cd517c55100fa7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/6/6/e66635a12d556f2927a072004f27cdda022adcb5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"${n \\choose m}&gt;n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -23px\" width=\"81\" height=\"55\" />.\n</p><p>Proof: Assume that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/9/8/69831ace095b556050195d53f2988c27b965330e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m&lt;\\frac{n-1}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"88\" height=\"38\" />.\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/6/e/a6ec0db174f3b705df19623976bf4b88ff502f3a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\Leftrightarrow$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"9\" /> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/5/8/358741e511a5ce68262400717af4175f2eb63b6e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m+1&lt;n-m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"122\" height=\"15\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/6/e/a6ec0db174f3b705df19623976bf4b88ff502f3a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\Leftrightarrow$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"9\" /> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/7/4/a74e388d9ef0c076fba83ca5655fc7042a9c6c6a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(m+1)!(n-m-1)!&lt;m!(n-m)!$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"281\" height=\"20\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/6/e/a6ec0db174f3b705df19623976bf4b88ff502f3a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\Leftrightarrow$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"9\" /> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/0/d/f0dae5d34bbbb8d55468ae449ec97693c398e39a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{n!}{m!(n-m)!}&lt;\\frac{n!}{(m+1)!(n-m-1)!}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"289\" height=\"44\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/6/e/a6ec0db174f3b705df19623976bf4b88ff502f3a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\Leftrightarrow$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"9\" /> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/4/e/a4ec0837421f2adff2b8fc35b7197f7279c9223b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"${n \\choose m}&lt;{n \\choose m+1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -23px\" width=\"146\" height=\"55\" />\n</p><p>Similarly, we can prove that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/e/f/9ef2f339fcfd080c2462a03cb28826897cb9390e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"${n \\choose m}&gt;{n \\choose m+1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -23px\" width=\"146\" height=\"55\" /> for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/6/5/4658af68182ef81fad288e37d704e496841e1e8e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m&gt;\\frac{n-1}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"88\" height=\"38\" />.\n</p><p>Now we split our proof into two cases.\n</p><p>Case 1: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> is even.\n</p><p>The largest integer less than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/8/1/e81417cdf462da47d08c54d86bd14a4b25c723d1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{n-1}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"44\" height=\"37\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/1/091aff55f00d0bc9cdc3c2d200c565ab1f4a212c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{n}{2}-1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"48\" height=\"34\" />, so we know that:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/d/a/7da871a6424456023c773516d2df126ca72d4042.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"${n \\choose \\frac{n}{2}}&gt;{n \\choose \\frac{n}{2}-1}&gt;\\cdot\\cdot\\cdot&gt;{n \\choose 2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -23px\" width=\"249\" height=\"55\" />\n</p><p>by induction. On the other hand, the smallest integer greater than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/8/1/e81417cdf462da47d08c54d86bd14a4b25c723d1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{n-1}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"44\" height=\"37\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/2/7/f275b1f33e3c37ece0e41964f0bcb806e95b46b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{n}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"13\" height=\"33\" />, so we know that:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/1/4/2141162ad61172b0916542e50158dfbd2880c530.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"${n \\choose \\frac{n}{2}}&gt;{n \\choose \\frac{n}{2}+1}&gt;\\cdot\\cdot\\cdot&gt;{n \\choose n-2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -23px\" width=\"280\" height=\"55\" />\n</p><p>also by induction. Thus out of the given range for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/0/f5047d1e0cbb50ec208923a22cd517c55100fa7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"10\" /> we know that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/2/e/42efa0bbb00cc7380115e9c67af705b54bfec3e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"${n \\choose 2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -23px\" width=\"41\" height=\"55\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/b/2/ab23ca17ab9d7a4491a6dcfedfea68aed22ef32c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"${n \\choose n-2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -23px\" width=\"72\" height=\"55\" /> are the minimum values, and all that is left is to prove that they are both greater than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />. Furthermore, since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/3/2/f32e391742ae681f642db18db9f91d99b38fc6a8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"${n \\choose 2}={n \\choose n-2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -23px\" width=\"135\" height=\"55\" />, we only have to prove that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/1/f/71fff9687ad8046fc6747791d4bd9d901abebf25.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"${n \\choose 2}&gt;n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -23px\" width=\"76\" height=\"55\" />.\n</p><p>We start with the given: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/0/d/70d8a348130d140aad5a9c23958578539f753241.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n&gt;3$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"43\" height=\"13\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/6/e/a6ec0db174f3b705df19623976bf4b88ff502f3a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\Leftrightarrow$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"9\" /> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/8/b/08b6549c26bac7c64c92aff623a4887d318dbd14.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{n-1}{2}&gt;1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"81\" height=\"38\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/6/e/a6ec0db174f3b705df19623976bf4b88ff502f3a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\Leftrightarrow$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"9\" /> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/2/ff2cf05f85e84dc6a8d469194ef165929fe9ba1f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}&gt;n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"108\" height=\"40\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/6/e/a6ec0db174f3b705df19623976bf4b88ff502f3a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\Leftrightarrow$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"9\" /> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/0/f/00f8101e8abf1aaa477acdfa74c4b4dbcd02217a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{n!}{2!(n-2)!}&gt;n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"117\" height=\"44\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/6/e/a6ec0db174f3b705df19623976bf4b88ff502f3a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\Leftrightarrow$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"9\" /> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/1/f/71fff9687ad8046fc6747791d4bd9d901abebf25.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"${n \\choose 2}&gt;n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -23px\" width=\"76\" height=\"55\" />\n</p><p>Thus we have proven the inequality for all even <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />.\n</p><p>Case 2: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> is odd.\n</p><p>The greatest integer less than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/8/1/e81417cdf462da47d08c54d86bd14a4b25c723d1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{n-1}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"44\" height=\"37\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/d/b/fdbfa01dfd6a795da947b45ab952576c38417ab3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{n-3}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"48\" height=\"38\" />, so we know that:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/3/f/e3f9ffb86d1b575c43ee134fc48e50d1f1f2d900.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"${n \\choose \\frac{n-1}{2}}&gt;{n \\choose \\frac{n-3}{2}}&gt;\\cdot\\cdot\\cdot&gt;{n \\choose 2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -23px\" width=\"251\" height=\"55\" />\n</p><p>by induction. On the other hand, the smallest integer greater than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/8/1/e81417cdf462da47d08c54d86bd14a4b25c723d1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{n-1}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"44\" height=\"37\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/f/36ff83257ab4ee6b8fa29edf847deb73d5778815.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{n+1}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"44\" height=\"37\" />, so we know that:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/e/2/de23928b3d38fc57f14854a2d6c3efda67dd8764.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"${n \\choose \\frac{n+1}{2}}&gt;{n \\choose \\frac{n+3}{2}}&gt;\\cdot\\cdot\\cdot&gt;{n \\choose n-2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -23px\" width=\"282\" height=\"55\" />\n</p><p>also by induction. Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/9/d/49d1cd1662f6833da1fe3341dd027849e2944760.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"${n \\choose \\frac{n+1}{2}}={n \\choose \\frac{n-1}{2}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -23px\" width=\"141\" height=\"55\" />, we know that once again, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/9/7/b97f40c8b9baa2ed401491964b02b9daccd6f9a1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"${n \\choose n-2}={n \\choose 2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -23px\" width=\"135\" height=\"55\" /> is the minimum of the given range for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/0/f5047d1e0cbb50ec208923a22cd517c55100fa7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"10\" />, and the same proof applies. Thus, the inequality holds true for odd and in turn all positive integers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/0/d/70d8a348130d140aad5a9c23958578539f753241.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n&gt;3$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"43\" height=\"13\" />.\n</p><p><br />\nAs a result, due to our lemma, there are always more permutations of the columns than the number of columns itself, so there will always exist a permutation of the column such that there are no corresponding original columns of which to match with. Thus there are no solutions for this case.\n</p><p><br />\nIn conclusion, there are a total of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/3/8/538bb12b0aa02eb9313e523fa163f05ea8a238a8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2\\cdot n!+2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"71\" height=\"16\" /> different colorings for which the above apply.\n</p><p><br />\n~ <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=User:Eevee9406\" title=\"User:Eevee9406\">eevee9406</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2\">Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_USAJMO_Problems/Problem_4&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Lemma 1: Each row and column must have the same number of red squares.\n</p><p>Proof: Suppose two rows do not have the same number of red squares. Suppose Rowan permutes the two rows onto each other. Then, because the two rows have a different number of red squares, there is no way for Colin to permute the columns, which permutes the squares in the row, to match the other row because the two rows have a different number of red squares. By a similar proof, the columns must have the same number of red squares as each other. Assume that each row has <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/0/f5047d1e0cbb50ec208923a22cd517c55100fa7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"10\" /> red squares. Then, there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/d/c/0dc1fb204e01ca7b63559f5c089987150a119138.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$nm$\" width=\"26\" height=\"8\" /> red squares in total. Because each column must have the same number of red squares as the other columns, the columns also must have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/0/f5047d1e0cbb50ec208923a22cd517c55100fa7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"10\" /> red squares each.\n</p><p><br />\nNow, we want to find which values of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/0/f5047d1e0cbb50ec208923a22cd517c55100fa7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"10\" /> can produce a valid orderly coloring.\n</p><p><br />\nLemma: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/9/c/59cb103837f65812b805af39dd82130c8be15b77.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C(n,k)&gt;n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"94\" height=\"20\" /> for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/a/0/fa086170c9ecb3e8cd0fd931d76076ff1798bed1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k\\neq 0,1,n-1,n.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"137\" height=\"18\" /> This is needed to prove the next lemma and is the most mathematical part of the solution.\n</p><p>Proof: Start with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/5/1/c5199d5c9e3f8ca17ec7b42f825ea6048a3f22ad.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C(n,1)=n.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"99\" height=\"20\" /> Each time we increase <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" /> by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> until <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/2/7/527cd24c0a60e4441e4fa21c80e89cd222ef569c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k=\\lfloor0.5n\\rfloor,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"91\" height=\"20\" /> we are multiplying the previous value of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/9/4/894cb483b297d8865c46151af7ceeaba9a2593e3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C(n,k)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"59\" height=\"20\" /> by a fraction more than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/e/4/5e45606a040af22c2ac21fb18a47ba12bd081ed8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1.\\text{*}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"24\" height=\"14\" /> Thus, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/9/c/59cb103837f65812b805af39dd82130c8be15b77.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C(n,k)&gt;n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"94\" height=\"20\" /> for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/a/e/8ae7a2a5ca4ac9d6104bf794e4e96b189e25a9e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2\\le n \\le \\lfloor0.5n\\rfloor.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"126\" height=\"20\" /> Because <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/e/d/4ed4069c1b0f4babd193b08fbae39b090bb1447f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C(n,k)=C(n,n-k),$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"179\" height=\"20\" /> the lemma is proven for the rest of the conjectured values. \n</p><p><br />\nLemma 2: If <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/1/e/51e044558d7370e7afeb92e17247e2bb5dfce2db.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m\\neq 0,1,n-1,n,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"143\" height=\"18\" /> the coloring is not orderly.\n</p><p>Proof: Let's take a look at a particular column, where assume there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/f/3/af34ecc3c5c5841fb7a372ed254755536caf8c6c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2\\le m\\le n-2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"118\" height=\"16\" /> red squares. When Rowan permutes the rows, the squares in this column have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/b/d/3bd4a329fa69e0153942d456f363bdd8ebe1e59b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C(n,m)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"65\" height=\"20\" /> possible permutations. In order for Colin to be able permute the columns to return the grid to its original state, all of these <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/b/d/3bd4a329fa69e0153942d456f363bdd8ebe1e59b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C(n,m)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"65\" height=\"20\" /> permutations must be columns of the original grid. However, because there are only <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> columns. By the previous lemma, there are more permutations than columns, so the we are done.\n</p><p><br />\nLemma 3: If a permutation satisfies <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/3/6/a362a4be5dca70fee8ef0d1184f6ac95950bfe36.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m=0,1,n-1,n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"138\" height=\"17\" /> and satisfies lemma <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/b/f/5bf27edd6ee0c25241392922eca9b9c9144f4d73.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"16\" height=\"17\" /> it is orderly.\n</p><p>Proof: The <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/9/c/a9c5eab3ffc218c18c355b2d5e7c71211badafe9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m=0,n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"70\" height=\"17\" /> case is trivial: the grid never changes! For <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/e/d/ded8fadf85c36a6d1ca9a56474de2db5a0f4f46d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m=1,n-1,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"106\" height=\"17\" /> we only have to prove the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/d/9/1d96ecc15129c5631ba4a2c6578e91e3d13e3baf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n=1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"46\" height=\"14\" /> case because of symmetry: we can change red to blue and blue to red and it won't change the orderliness of the coloring as the overall arrangement of the different colors won't be changed. For the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/d/5/cd5414931a31e0338eedfb94406f6c9445341e46.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m=1 case,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"91\" height=\"17\" /> when Rowan permutes the rows, all of the columns will still be different columns of the original grid (as each column must have one red square, the rows of the red squares will be different) and can be permuted back to the original arrangement by Colin. Thus, this lemma is true.\n</p><p><br />\nFinally, there is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> coloring for the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/e/2/2e275495e7e44d279fb3e5051b3a63115e79ead5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m=0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"51\" height=\"14\" /> case, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/3/f/63f344e7d8b14a7886fc7fb72da53cd0a3271dcc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n!$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" /> ways to permute the red squares to see which column values are assigned to the rows in the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/4/b/a4b5800f5ad040d308609190ef18a9e0ca08a9b0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m=1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"51\" height=\"14\" /> case. There are similarly <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/3/f/63f344e7d8b14a7886fc7fb72da53cd0a3271dcc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n!$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" /> colorings for the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/f/6/af644ae994acd3a0a7d07b14d53601d1eeed4a77.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m=n-1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"84\" height=\"15\" /> case, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> coloring for the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/e/2/2e275495e7e44d279fb3e5051b3a63115e79ead5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m=0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"51\" height=\"14\" /> case. Thus, the answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/6/2/86260b86e14d677bd8b648b2baf036bc0e043218.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{2n!+2}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -8px\" width=\"75\" height=\"27\" />\n</p><p><br />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/6/6/766ccee482aeea5fd9e61b55e573f36a1a8a4946.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{*}$\" style=\"vertical-align: 5px\" width=\"7\" height=\"7\" /> Specifically, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/5/8c5bd44e02d93d108f108d435df660dde26381a3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{C(n,k)}{C(n,k-1)}=\\frac{n-k+1}{k-1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"197\" height=\"45\" /> which is greater than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/a/e/8ae7a2a5ca4ac9d6104bf794e4e96b189e25a9e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2\\le n \\le \\lfloor0.5n\\rfloor.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"126\" height=\"20\" />\n</p><p><br />\n~BS2012\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Another_way_to_prove_that\">Another way to prove that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/b/0/8b08a9598c0e544ced8c2a6a947204a2af840f2f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\binom{n}{m}&gt;n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -23px\" width=\"81\" height=\"55\" /></span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_USAJMO_Problems/Problem_4&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Another way to prove that\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Suppose we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/f/f5f094534990ccc8400cb0360cb9f9f477ac4cf5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2\\le{m}\\le\\lfloor{\\frac{n}{2}}\\rfloor$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"106\" height=\"34\" />. Our goal is to show that\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/7/c/d7c8efad2e56fe1925cbfbec1828fe4d34e63a25.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\binom{n}{m}&gt;n\\]\" width=\"74\" height=\"53\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/1/f/11f7bcd669f23cbd22ab80b45168d2ecf05bf692.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{n}{m}\\cdot\\frac{n-1}{m-1}\\cdot\\frac{n-2}{m-2}\\cdots\\frac{n-m+1}{1}&gt;n\\]\" width=\"293\" height=\"37\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/3/3/533571bce1cd24d84a5d3d9726b57cc5a9576711.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{1}{m}\\cdot\\frac{n-1}{m-1}\\cdot\\frac{n-2}{m-2}\\cdots\\frac{n-m+1}{1}&gt;1\\]\" width=\"290\" height=\"37\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/7/9/779bffae963f74fc59d112bfa5a5b74ba852677c.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{n-1}{m-1}\\cdot\\frac{n-2}{m-2}\\cdots\\frac{n-m+1}{m}&gt;1\\]\" width=\"257\" height=\"37\" />.\n</p><p>Each of the above fractions is greater than one because <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/f/f5f094534990ccc8400cb0360cb9f9f477ac4cf5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2\\le{m}\\le\\lfloor{\\frac{n}{2}}\\rfloor$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"106\" height=\"34\" />, so we are done.\n</p><p>~alexanderruan\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_USAJMO_Problems/Problem_4&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:40%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_USAJMO\" title=\"2024 USAJMO\">2024 USAJMO</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_USAJMO_Problems\" title=\"2024 USAJMO Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"http://www.artofproblemsolving.com/Forum/resources.php?c=182&amp;cid=176&amp;year=2024\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_USAJMO_Problems/Problem_3\" title=\"2024 USAJMO Problems/Problem 3\">Problem 3</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_USAJMO_Problems/Problem_5\" title=\"2024 USAJMO Problems/Problem 5\">Problem 5</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_USAJMO_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"2024 USAJMO Problems/Problem 1\">1</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_USAJMO_Problems/Problem_2\" title=\"2024 USAJMO Problems/Problem 2\">2</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_USAJMO_Problems/Problem_3\" title=\"2024 USAJMO Problems/Problem 3\">3</a> <b>•</b> <a class=\"mw-selflink selflink\">4</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_USAJMO_Problems/Problem_5\" title=\"2024 USAJMO Problems/Problem 5\">5</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_USAJMO_Problems/Problem_6\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"2024 USAJMO Problems/Problem 6\">6</a>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=USAJMO_Problems_and_Solutions\" title=\"USAJMO Problems and Solutions\"> All USAJMO Problems and Solutions</a></b>\n</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<div class=\"print\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;width:100%;justify-content:space-between;align-items:center\">\n<div>\n<p><span class=\"mw-default-size\" typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:AMC_Logo.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/d/d1/AMC_Logo.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"97\" height=\"84\" class=\"mw-file-element\" /></a></span>\n</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex-grow:1;text-align:right\">\n<p>These problems are copyrighted &#169; by the <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://www.maa.org\">Mathematical Association of America</a>. \n</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"clear:both;\"></div>\n</div></div>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261018030615\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: [show‐toc]\nCPU time usage: 0.060 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.082 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 664/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 1326/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 48/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 3/100\nExpensive parser function coun